  "result": "I fixed the bug and all 8 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `save_order` in `shop/storage.py:17` filtered the existing rows the wrong way round:\n\n```python\nrows = [r for r in self.load() if r[\"id\"] == order.id]   # before\nrows = [r for r in self.load() if r[\"id\"] != order.id]   # after\n```\n\nThe filter was supposed to remove any earlier copy of the order being saved, so that saving it again updates it. Because it used `==`, it kept *only* rows with that same id. Every save therefore erased all other orders, and the file ended up holding just the last one saved.\n\n**Test:** I added `tests/test_storage.py` with two tests:\n- **Different orders:** saving two different orders keeps both.\n- **Same order twice:** saving one order again replaces its row instead of adding a second copy, and the new state is stored.\n\nBoth tests fail against the original `storage.py` and pass with the fix. I didn't commit anything.",
